<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Lavo Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">648 CE – 1388 CE (approx.) · capital Lopburi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A kingdom of the lower Chao Phraya centred on Lopburi, first a Dvaravati city, then a Khmer provincial seat, and a forerunner of Ayutthaya.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lavo emerged as one of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mon-people">Mon</a> towns of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dvaravati">Dvaravati</a> world, with Lopburi as its centre from about the 7th century. Chronicles say <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chamadevi">Chamadevi</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hariphunchai">Hariphunchai</a> was a Lavo princess. In the 10th and 11th centuries Lavo came under the control of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> rule from the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suryavarman-i">Suryavarman I</a>, Lopburi was a provincial capital with temples such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phra-prang-sam-yot">Phra Prang Sam Yot</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wat-phra-si-rattana-mahathat-lopburi">Wat Phra Si Rattana Mahathat</a> showing the Khmer style. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahayana-buddhism">Mahayana Buddhism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> cults coexisted with older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> traditions. Lavo also sent its own missions to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> court in the 12th century, and to the Yuan in the late 13th, suggesting periods of independence.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> power waned in the 13th century, Lavo was linked by marriage and politics with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/suphannaphum">Suphannaphum</a>, and in 1351 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramathibodi-i">U Thong</a> founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya</a>, combining both. Lopburi remained a secondary royal city, used by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">King Narai</a> as a residence.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/lavo-kingdom.jpg" alt="Lavo Kingdom" width="320" height="451"><figcaption>Image: Javierfv1212 · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map-of-southeast-asia_1000_-_1100_CE.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>648 CE – 1388 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lopburi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Mon, Old Khmer</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lavo, Lopburi, Louvo</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1018&amp;focus=lavo-kingdom" aria-label="Lavo Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7431 1627 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lavo Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7751 1883L7732 1876M7751 1883L7846 1933M7751 1883L7752 1900M7751 1883L7682 1744"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7732 1876h0M7846 1933h0M7752 1900h0M7682 1744h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7751 1883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7751 1883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lopburi. <a href="/atlas?year=1018&amp;focus=lavo-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
